PRD

PRD — The Lenny Growth Assistant
1. User
Primary user: An internal product/growth team member (PM, growth marketer, or founder) at a
company that follows Lenny’s Podcast for tactical guidance, who needs quick, trustworthy answers to
product and growth questions without re-listening to hours of episodes.
Secondary user: The technical evaluator assessing this take-home — needs to understand, run, and
verify the system quickly (this shapes several product decisions below: visible provider indicator, visible
sources, health endpoints).
2. Problem
Lenny’s Podcast contains hundreds of hours of high-quality product/growth advice, but it’s locked in
unstructured audio/transcript form. Teams currently either don’t use this knowledge at all, or waste time
manually searching transcripts / re-listening to find the one relevant segment. There’s no way to ask a
direct question and get a grounded, sourced answer, let alone turn that answer into a polished piece of
written content.
3. User Jobs-to-be-Done
“When I’m making a product/growth decision, I want to quickly check what respected practitioners
have said about this exact situation, so I can borrow their reasoning instead of guessing.”
“When I find a good insight, I want to turn it into a shareable written artifact (essay, doc) without
doing the writing from scratch.”
“When I get an answer, I want to know it’s actually grounded in something real, not the model
making it up.”
4. Pain Points Removed
No efficient way to search/query podcast transcripts today.
Risk of misremembering or misattributing advice.
Time cost of turning a good insight into publishable writing.
No visibility into why an AI assistant said what it said (source opacity).
5. Success Metrics
Primary (product): ≥90% of answered questions include at least one verifiable transcript citation
(measures grounding, not just fluency).
Secondary (operational): Fewer than 5% of chat requests result in an unhandled error (timeout,
provider failure, DB failure) reaching the user as a raw stack trace — measures resilience per §5 of
the assignment.
Tertiary (content quality): Generated Ship 30 for 30 essays land within 1,150–1,350 words
(≈1,250 target) with zero unsupported factual claims flagged in manual review.
6. Assumptions
(Documented because the client brief — the take-home assignment — was intentionally underspecified in
these areas.)
1. Agent framework: Assignment allows Claude Agent SDK or Pi Coding Agent. We use the Claude

Agent SDK / tool-use pattern, chosen for documentation maturity and because it pairs naturally
with Claude as the cloud provider.
2. Cloud LLM provider: Default is Anthropic Claude, with the provider abstraction built so OpenAI
(or others) can be added without touching call sites.
3. Local model: We target a small-to-mid Ollama model (e.g., llama3.1:8b class) sized to run on a
typical developer laptop, acknowledging lower quality than the cloud model — documented as a
trade-off, not hidden.
4. “User metadata”: Since there’s no auth system specified, we treat this as a lightweight,
anonymous per-browser client identifier (not PII), stored alongside sessions — sufficient for
demo/evaluation purposes, explicitly not production-grade auth.
5. Database hosting: Default is local Postgres via Docker Compose for one-command
reproducibility; Supabase/Railway documented as optional per the assignment’s “may use.”
6. Conversation history: Not explicitly mandatory, but included (a session sidebar) since it’s a normal
expectation of “start a new chat” language in §3.1.

7. Transcript repository structure: We assume the linked GitHub repo ( ChatPRD/lennys-podcast-
transcripts ) contains per-episode text/markdown transcript files; ingestion code is written

defensively and documented as needing a first-run verification against the actual repo contents,
since this sandbox cannot fetch it directly.
7. Scope (In)
Grounded Q&A over Lenny’s transcripts (RAG), with citations.
Follow-up question handling within a session.
Ship 30 for 30 essay generation skill.
Markdown + HTML/CSS artifact generation with in-app Artifact Viewer.
Session + message persistence in PostgreSQL.
Cloud (Claude) + local (Ollama) provider switching via config.
Docker Compose one-command startup.
Structured logging and graceful degradation for the failure modes listed in §5 of the assignment.
8. Scope (Out)
User authentication/accounts — out of scope; anonymous sessions only. Why: not requested,
and would add significant surface area (auth flows, password storage, security review) without
serving the evaluation goal.
Multi-tenant / team workspaces — out of scope. Why: single-user demo product per the
assignment framing.
Fine-tuning or training custom models — out of scope. Why: assignment specifies provider
integration, not model training.
Full production-grade vector database (e.g., managed pgvector cluster) — out of scope; we
use a lightweight local vector index. Why: prioritizes one-command reproducibility over retrieval
sophistication; documented as an upgrade path.
Real-time transcript refresh/webhooks — out of scope; ingestion is a manual/scripted re-run.
Why: transcripts don’t change frequently enough to justify streaming ingestion infrastructure for a
demo.
9. User Flows
1. New session → grounded question: User opens app → new session auto-created → asks a
product/growth question → assistant retrieves relevant transcript chunks → answers with inline
source citations → user asks a follow-up → context preserved.
2. Unsupported question: User asks something transcripts don’t cover → assistant explicitly states
the knowledge base doesn’t support an answer, rather than inventing one.
3. Ship 30 essay generation: User asks for an essay on a topic discussed in the transcripts → Ship30
skill retrieves grounded material → generates ~1,250-word essay with
hook/headings/bullets/takeaway → renders as a Markdown artifact in the Artifact Viewer.
4. HTML artifact generation: User asks for a rendered snippet (e.g., a one-pager) → assistant
generates sanitized HTML/CSS → Artifact Viewer renders it in a sandboxed iframe beside the chat.

5. Provider switch: Evaluator changes MODEL_PROVIDER env var from claude to ollama , restarts → UI
provider indicator updates → same flows work against the local model.
6. Failure path: Ollama is stopped / DB is down / API key missing → user gets a clear, non-technical
error message; app doesn’t crash; logs capture the real cause.
10. Acceptance Criteria
A new chat session can be created and maintains isolated context from other sessions.
At least 90% of grounded answers (manual sample of 10 questions) include a specific, verifiable
transcript source.
Asking a question outside the knowledge base produces an explicit “not supported” response, not a
fabricated answer.
Ship 30 essay output is within the target word range and includes headings, bullets, bold emphasis,
and a stated takeaway.
Markdown and HTML artifacts render inside the Artifact Viewer without navigating away from the
app.
Injected <script> content in a generated/user-influenced HTML artifact does not execute in the
viewer.
Switching MODEL_PROVIDER between claude and ollama requires no code change and is reflected in
the UI/config.
Stopping Postgres, stopping Ollama, or removing the API key each produce a graceful,
understandable error rather than a crash or raw stack trace.
docker compose up --build brings up a working system on a clean checkout (Ollama model pull
remains a manual step, documented).
